  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry conditions with above normal temperatures \npersist through the weekend. This will exacerbate ongoing water\nresource drought conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA northern stream shortwave passes today, but the low levels are\ntoo dry for any precipitation. Deep layered ridging builds in\ntonight and Wednesday with associated subsidence keeping things\ndry. At the surface, high pressure builds in to the southwest\nthrough Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere may be a brief chance for some precipitation early next \nweek before high pressure returns. Any drought relief is \nunlikely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA quick moving cold front is expected to pass through the \nregion during the day on Monday, bringing potential for much \nneeded showers across the area. However, between the speed of \nthe front and the lack of significant moisture available, \nrainfall totals will be meager with eastern Maine and Downeast \nhaving the best chances for showers. \n\nThere is still a fair amount of uncertainty. At the same time \nas the frontal passage, a coastal low will be working its way \nnorth and is forecast to get swept out to sea when the front \ncatches up to the low. However, if the low moves faster than \ncurrent forecasts predict, higher rainfall totals could be \npossible, especially Downeast.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage there is a strong agreement among\nensembles that high pressure will move in from Quebec bringing\nwith it cold and dry air that will dominate the weather pattern\nthrough Thanksgiving.",
